answer:
Plain data parallelism (DDP) keeps a full copy of everything on every GPU. ZeRO removes the copies by giving each GPU one shard (a 1/N slice) of the model states. Any GPU that needs another shard gets it through a collective (a communication operation all GPUs take part in) just before it needs it. FSDP is PyTorch's implementation of ZeRO's fully sharded mode, stage 3. Neither paper is in your library; I took both from arXiv.

**Why it matters.** With mixed-precision Adam, each parameter costs 16 bytes of "model state": 2 for the fp16 weights, 2 for the fp16 gradients, and 12 for the optimizer. Those 12 are an fp32 master copy, the momentum and the variance, at 4 bytes each ([ZeRO, p. 8](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1910.02054)). DDP stores all 16Ψ bytes on every GPU, where Ψ is the parameter count. The 12Ψ of optimizer state is the largest share, so ZeRO shards it first.

**What changes at each stage.** Per-GPU model-state memory with N GPUs, from the ZeRO paper (pp. 2–3, Fig. 1):

| Strategy | What is sharded | Memory per GPU | Traffic per GPU per step |
|---|---|---|---|
| DDP | nothing | 16Ψ | 2Ψ (all-reduce) |
| ZeRO-1 (P_os) | optimizer states | 4Ψ + 12Ψ/N | 2Ψ, same as DP |
| ZeRO-2 (P_os+g) | + gradients | 2Ψ + 14Ψ/N | 2Ψ, same as DP |
| ZeRO-3 / FSDP (P_os+g+p) | + parameters | 16Ψ/N | 3Ψ, 1.5× DP |

The paper's example is a 7.5B-parameter model on 64 GPUs. By my arithmetic from these formulas, that is 120 GB → 31.4 GB → 16.6 GB → 1.9 GB per GPU. The diagram uses N = 4 to keep it readable.

**Things to watch as you step through:**

1. **Gradient sync.** DDP all-reduces the gradients, so every GPU ends up with the full summed gradient. The ZeRO stages reduce-scatter instead: each GPU receives only the summed slice for the shard it owns, which is all its optimizer needs. In ZeRO-2 and ZeRO-3 this happens bucket by bucket during the backward pass. That is why the full gradient never exists on any GPU.
2. **Optimizer step.** DDP runs the same Adam update four times, once per GPU. Under ZeRO each GPU updates only its own shard.
3. **The extra 1Ψ in ZeRO-3.** ZeRO-1 and ZeRO-2 keep full parameters, so after the optimizer step they all-gather the updated shards back. ZeRO-3 never keeps full parameters. Instead it all-gathers them once before forward, frees them, and gathers them again before backward. Two all-gathers plus one reduce-scatter make 3Ψ. FSDP's paper reports the same 1.5× overhead over DDP with ring collectives ([FSDP, p. 4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2304.11277)).

**How FSDP does it.** You wrap groups of layers into "FSDP units". Each unit's parameters are flattened into one `FlatParameter` and split evenly across ranks. Forward gathers one unit, runs it, and frees the borrowed shards before moving to the next unit. Backward gathers again, then reduce-scatters that unit's gradients (FSDP, pp. 4–5). So peak parameter memory is your own shards plus the largest single unit. Wrapping at a finer granularity lowers that peak but issues more, smaller collectives, which is FSDP's main memory-versus-throughput trade-off (p. 5).

The diagram draws the whole model as one unit, so the ZeRO-3 forward and backward steps show everything gathered at once. The "+ gathered" readout marks this temporary memory. It is not resident memory.


