| Strategy | What each GPU computes | Communication needed |
|---|---|---|
| **Tensor (TP)** | The **same tokens**, using different weight-matrix shards. In the illustrated MLP, each GPU computes different intermediate features, then a partial output. | **All-reduce:** sum partial outputs and give the complete result to every GPU. Backward also sums contributions to input gradients. [1] |
| **Context (CP)** | **Different positions of the same sequence**, using replicated weights. Each GPU owns local queries but needs other positions’ keys and values. | **KV exchange:** the illustrated ring streams KV blocks between GPUs. Backward exchanges KV again and reduces their gradient contributions; replicated weights also need gradient synchronization. [2] |
| **Expert (EP)** | **Different complete experts**, processing whichever tokens the router assigns them—not necessarily consecutive tokens. | **All-to-all dispatch:** send activations to expert owners. **All-to-all combine:** return results to token owners. Backward routes activation gradients through corresponding exchanges. [3] |

The important distinction: **TP combines partial calculations; CP supplies missing context; EP moves tokens to the selected computation.**

These communication patterns are representative, not universal. TP with *sequence parallelism* uses **all-gather/reduce-scatter** around TP regions; CP has alternatives to the illustrated ring. The strategies can also be combined. [2–3]

